# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #2I - The ridge is a real defect
# ===========================================================================
#
# An entry recording 'about 1e-7' turned out to understate the error by four decades, because the sweep that produced it stopped one row short.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# The oldest open item in the project's own list read: 'a ridge near
# z/nu ~ 1.3, where Y reaches about 1e-7 at moderate order'. A later stage
# had extended an expansion across exactly that region, so the first
# question was whether the item had already been closed without anyone
# re-checking.
#
# It had not. The entry was wrong in the OTHER direction.
#
# MEASURED, against the vendored Cephes reference:
#
#     nu = 20.5, z/nu = 1.48    relative error 7.78e-8     (matches 'about 1e-7')
#     nu = 36.8, z/nu = 1.30    relative error 2.18
#     nu = 36.8, z/nu = 1.48    relative error 3.09e4
#
# The 1e-7 figure was simply the value at nu = 20.5, where whatever sweep
# produced it stopped. One row further and it is not an inaccuracy at all -
# the answer is WRONG.
#
# A DISCREPANCY NAMES NO CULPRIT. We disagreed with Cephes by a factor of
# 3e4 - and two earlier stages had both ended with CEPHES as the looser
# party, so precedent pointed the wrong way. The J-Y Wronskian, whose
# right-hand side is elementary, settled it: our residual 3.7e-3 against
# Cephes's 2.2e-23. Cephes is right here. Having been right before is not
# evidence.
#
# THE CAUSE IS IN THE INGREDIENT, NOT THE COMBINATION. For non-integer
# order,
#
#     Y_nu = [J_nu cos(nu pi) - J_(-nu)] / sin(nu pi)
#
# and J_(-36.8) at z = 54.5 comes from an ascending series whose terms reach
# exp(54) ~ 3e23 to produce a result of order 0.1. That matters practically:
# a cancellation guard measured AT THE SUBTRACTION - the idiom used
# everywhere else in this crate - would not have seen it, because by then
# both operands are already garbage.
#
# WHAT IS AND IS NOT AFFECTED. The ESTIMATE-SELECTING entry point is
# unaffected and accurate to 4.8e-13 at exactly those points, because it
# compares error estimates across several routes and lands on one that
# works. It is the single raw route that fails.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is posim notebook source. Lines beginning with '#' are
# comments and are ignored by the interpreter; every other line is a command
# that will be executed in order.
#
# From the repository root (the directory holding Cargo.toml), run ONE of:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2I.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --script   StageNbks/Stage_2I.posim
#
#   --notebook  prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript, like a Mathematica
#               notebook. This is the mode to use when reading along.
#   --script    prints the same transcript; the two differ only in how the
#               prompt lines are laid out. Either is reproducible.
#
# To work through it by hand instead, start the interactive interpreter:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release
#
# and paste the command lines one at a time. Type HELP for the full command
# language, and %quit to leave.
#
# First run will compile the workspace (a few minutes). Later runs are
# immediate. If 'cargo' is not found, install Rust from https://rustup.rs .
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BROWSER GUI, AND WHY IT IS NOT USED BY THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# posim has a live 3-D scene window served over HTTP and viewed in a browser.
# THIS NOTEBOOK DOES NOT OPEN IT, and you should not try to, because it would
# be empty. The reason is exact:
#
#   The window renders SIMULATOR BODIES - points, spheres, cuboids, tori,
#   disks, cylinders, dumbbells - by sending their positions each frame.
#   This notebook creates none. Running 'scene create' here prints
#
#       showing 0 entities
#
#   and 'scene start' then advances a world containing nothing: the Start,
#   Pause, Stop and Reverse buttons all respond, and nothing moves, because
#   there is nothing to move.
#
# That is not a defect to be worked around. The subject of this notebook has
# no position and no time coordinate, so there is nothing for a renderer to
# draw. Its results are the numbers and messages printed by the commands
# below, and those ARE the output.
#
# IF YOU WANT A WORKING SCENE WINDOW, use a notebook that builds bodies -
# StageNbks/Stage_2C.posim (six bodies in a box) and
# StageNbks/Stage_2H.posim (Newton's cradle and a settling ball) both open
# one automatically and start it running. The complete window procedure is
# written out inside each of those files.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANIMATION: NOT POSSIBLE HERE, AND THE EXACT REASON WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This stage cannot be animated, and the cause is precise rather than a
# matter of effort:
#
#   The scene window renders SIMULATOR ENTITIES. Concretely, it draws the
#   bodies held in the PhysicalObjectSystem - points, spheres, cuboids,
#   tori, disks, cylinders and dumbbells - by reading their positions and
#   orientations each frame and sending them over a websocket.
#
#   This stage evaluates SPECIAL FUNCTIONS. A call such as
#   bessel_j_nu(0.5, 2.0) creates no body, has no position, and has no time
#   coordinate: it is a pure function of its arguments. There is therefore
#   nothing for the renderer to draw, and 'scene create' on this session
#   would open a window showing an empty grid and the world axes.
#
#   That is not a defect and there is no workaround inside the scene window.
#   A function of a complex variable is not a moving object.
#
# What you CAN watch instead, and how, is written in the section above: the
# numbers printed by the command lines below change as you vary the order
# and the argument, and that variation IS the phenomenon this stage is about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# READ THIS FIRST - IT DETERMINES WHAT YOU WILL SEE.
#
# There are TWO routes to Y in this codebase, and the language exposes only
# one of them:
#
#   * bessel_complex::bessel_y_nu   the single RAW route, which is the one
#                                   this stage found to be wrong. It is a
#                                   Rust-level function and is NOT reachable
#                                   from posim.
#   * bessel_cnu::bessel_y_cnu      the SELECTOR, which compares error
#                                   estimates across several routes and
#                                   picks one that works.
#
# The posim function 'bessel_y_nu(nu, z)' calls the SELECTOR. So every line
# below returns a CORRECT value - agreeing with Cephes to about 1e-13 even
# at the points where the raw route is wrong by 3.09e4. That is the stage's
# conclusion made visible: the defect exists, and the supported path routes
# around it.
#
# To observe the raw route's failure directly you must use Rust, because it
# is not exposed to the language. The test that pins it is
# 'the_selector_is_accurate_where_the_raw_reflection_is_not' in
# special_functions/src/bessel_cnu.rs; run it from the repository root with:
#
#     cargo test -p special_functions --release the_selector_is_accurate -- --nocapture
#
# The three points that characterise the defect. Each value below is the
# SELECTOR's answer; compare against the Wronskian identity, which needs no
# reference implementation.

# A point where the raw route is still fine: error about 7.5e-12.
bessel_y_nu(12.3, 16.6)

# The point the original entry recorded: error about 7.8e-8. Still usable,
# and the source of the 'about 1e-7' figure.
bessel_y_nu(20.5, 30.34)

# One row further out - the points where the RAW route is wrong by 2.18 and
# by 3.09e4. Through the language you get the SELECTOR, so these print
# correct values (compare with Cephes: 6.801992e-2 at the second one).
# The contrast between these correct numbers and the raw route's failure
# is exactly why the selector exists.
bessel_y_nu(36.8, 47.84)
bessel_y_nu(36.8, 54.464)

# The referee, at a point where the raw route still answers. Both sides of
#     J_(nu+1) Y_nu - J_nu Y_(nu+1) = 2/(pi z)
bessel_j_nu(13.3, 16.6) * bessel_y_nu(12.3, 16.6) - bessel_j_nu(12.3, 16.6) * bessel_y_nu(13.3, 16.6)
2 / (3.14159265358979 * 16.6)

# Those agree to about fifteen digits, which is what a healthy point looks
# like. The whole stage is about a region where that stopped being true
# while the routine kept answering confidently.

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #2I NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
